+# Best AI Tools for Restaurants 2026
+
+[![Awesome](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/sindresorhus/awesome@main/media/badge.svg)](https://github.com/sindresorhus/awesome)
+[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
+![Topics](https://img.shields.io/github/stars/GagnDeep/awesome-best-ai-tools-for-restaurants-2026?style=social)
+
+> The best AI tools for restaurants 2026 are not a single POS plugin or hype-filled SaaS dashboard. They are practical open source building blocks you can self-host from GitHub for menu OCR, guest messaging, ordering flows, internal knowledge search, and demand forecasting. This list only includes tools that exist as public GitHub repositories and are relevant to real restaurant operations.
+
+## Table of Contents
+- [TL;DR](#tldr)
+- [Why This List](#why-this-list)
+- [Open Source Tools](#open-source-tools)
+- [FAQ](#faq)
+- [Contributing](#contributing)
+- [License](#license)
+
+## TL;DR
+- `TastyIgniter` is the strongest restaurant-native OSS platform here if you need online ordering, reservations, and restaurant management in one stack.
+- `Chatwoot`, `Rasa`, `Pipecat`, `Flowise`, and `Langflow` are the best starting points for AI-assisted guest support, ordering flows, and front-desk automation.
+- `PaddleOCR`, `Tesseract`, `Surya`, `Marker`, and `Unstructured` are the most useful menu digitization and document-to-structured-data tools.
+- `Qdrant`, `Weaviate`, `Haystack`, and `LanceDB` are strong foundations for menu search, internal SOP retrieval, and recommendation systems.
+- `Prophet`, `Darts`, `StatsForecast`, and `MLForecast` are the most practical OSS options for demand forecasting, labor planning, and prep optimization.
+
+## Why This List
+Restaurants need AI tools that can survive bad menu PDFs, multilingual guests, messy operational documents, and demand swings across dayparts. Open source matters here because restaurants, franchise groups, and agencies often need self-hosting, source visibility, and the freedom to tailor workflows around ordering, reservations, kitchens, and support.
+
+There is also a real market gap: truly restaurant-native open source AI products are still limited on GitHub. To stay honest, this list mixes restaurant-specific repositories with adjacent OSS AI infrastructure that restaurants can deploy today for menu extraction, conversational ordering, forecasting, retrieval, and workflow automation.
+
+## Open Source Tools
+
+### Restaurant Ops And Guest Experience
+
+#### [TastyIgniter](https://github.com/tastyigniter/TastyIgniter)
+> **Description:** TastyIgniter is the clearest restaurant-specific foundation in this list. Its README positions it as a developer-friendly, open source platform for online ordering, table reservation, and restaurant management, aimed at helping offline restaurants and takeaway businesses reach online customers. It is customizable, extension-driven, and suited to multi-location deployments. While it is not an LLM framework by itself, it is the most relevant OSS operating layer to pair with AI features such as menu extraction, support chat, and forecasting. GitHub shows roughly 3.3k stars, PHP as the primary language, an MIT license, and activity on September 12, 2025.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/tastyigniter/TastyIgniter](https://github.com/tastyigniter/TastyIgniter)
+- **Stars:** 3.3k ⭐
+- **Language:** PHP
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** Sep 12, 2025
+- **Category:** restaurant-management, online-ordering
+- **Best for:** self-hosted restaurant ordering and reservations as the core system around AI features
+
+---
+
+#### [Chatwoot](https://github.com/chatwoot/chatwoot)
+> **Description:** Chatwoot describes itself as a modern, open source, self-hosted customer support platform and includes an AI support agent called Captain. The README emphasizes omnichannel support across live chat, email, social channels, messaging apps, and SMS, which maps well to restaurant guest care, reservation questions, delivery complaints, and event inquiries. It gives operators control over customer data while centralizing conversations in one inbox. For restaurants, that makes it useful as the service desk layer behind AI-assisted FAQ handling and escalation. GitHub shows about 26.7k stars, Ruby as the main language, an MIT license, and recent activity on January 2, 2026.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/chatwoot/chatwoot](https://github.com/chatwoot/chatwoot)
+- **Stars:** 26.7k ⭐
+- **Language:** Ruby
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** Jan 2, 2026
+- **Category:** support, guest-messaging
+- **Best for:** omnichannel guest support with AI-assisted responses and handoff
+
+---
+
+#### [Rasa](https://github.com/RasaHQ/rasa)
+> **Description:** Rasa Open Source is a mature conversational AI framework for building text and voice assistants with business logic and multi-channel integrations. Its README highlights contextual assistants, support for custom channels, and reliability for real-world flows, all of which are relevant to restaurant use cases like reservation assistants, catering intake, private dining qualification, and order status bots. Rasa is now in maintenance mode while the company pushes newer products, but the open source framework remains widely used and very capable. GitHub shows just over 20.9k stars, Python as the primary language, an Apache-2.0 license, and activity on December 18, 2025.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/RasaHQ/rasa](https://github.com/RasaHQ/rasa)
+- **Stars:** 20.9k ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** Dec 18, 2025
+- **Category:** conversational-ai, ordering-bots
+- **Best for:** structured restaurant chatbots that need explicit flows and business rules
+
+---
+
+#### [Pipecat](https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat)
+> **Description:** Pipecat is an open source Python framework for real-time voice and multimodal conversational agents. The README focuses on orchestrating audio, video, AI services, transports, and conversation pipelines so developers can build voice-first assistants, guided flows, and business agents. For restaurants, that makes it a practical base for phone ordering, drive-through experiments, kiosk voice UX, or multilingual guest intake. It is especially useful when latency and streaming speech matter more than simple web chat. GitHub shows roughly 9.8k stars, Python as the main language, a BSD-2-Clause license, and activity on January 5, 2026.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat](https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat)
+- **Stars:** 9.8k ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** BSD-2-Clause
+- **Last Commit:** Jan 5, 2026
+- **Category:** voice-ai, real-time-agents
+- **Best for:** phone and kiosk voice agents for restaurants
+
+---
+
+#### [Flowise](https://github.com/FlowiseAI/Flowise)
+> **Description:** Flowise is a visual builder for AI agents and workflows, with self-hosting and support for major models, vector stores, and RAG patterns. The README emphasizes drag-and-drop authoring, Docker deployment, environment-based configuration, and compatibility with common LLM tooling. That makes it well suited to restaurant teams or agencies that want to prototype staff copilots, menu Q&A, SOP assistants, and guest-facing workflows without building everything from scratch. It is a strong orchestration layer when restaurant operators need quick iteration on AI flows rather than a code-only framework. GitHub shows about 47.8k stars, TypeScript as the primary language, Apache-2.0 licensing, and activity on December 29, 2025.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/FlowiseAI/Flowise](https://github.com/FlowiseAI/Flowise)
+- **Stars:** 47.8k ⭐
+- **Language:** TypeScript
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** Dec 29, 2025
+- **Category:** workflow-automation, low-code
+- **Best for:** quickly building restaurant AI workflows and internal copilots
+
+---
+
+#### [Langflow](https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow)
+> **Description:** Langflow is a visual platform for building and deploying AI-powered agents and workflows. Its README stresses that every workflow can become an API or MCP-enabled tool, with support for major LLMs, vector databases, and tool integrations. For restaurant operators, this is useful when you want one visual environment to connect menu data, policy documents, reservation rules, and support workflows into a single application. Compared with lighter chatbot tools, Langflow is better when you need reusable components and integration hooks for production systems. GitHub shows about 143k stars, Python as the main language, an MIT license, and activity on January 3, 2026.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/langflow-ai/langflow](https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow)
+- **Stars:** 143k ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** Jan 3, 2026
+- **Category:** agent-workflows, orchestration
+- **Best for:** production-grade restaurant AI workflows with APIs and tool exposure
+
+---
+
+#### [AnythingLLM](https://github.com/Mintplex-Labs/anything-llm)
+> **Description:** AnythingLLM packages RAG, AI agents, an embeddable chat widget, document ingestion, multiple LLM backends, and vector database support into one self-hosted application. The README highlights no-code agent building, MCP support, multimodal capabilities, drag-and-drop documents, and a developer API for custom integrations. In restaurant contexts, it is useful for private internal knowledge bases, franchise ops manuals, HR question answering, and website assistants grounded on menus or policies. It is more of an application platform than a framework, which is valuable for teams that want working software quickly. GitHub shows roughly 53.3k stars, JavaScript as the primary language, an MIT license, and activity on January 14, 2026.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/Mintplex-Labs/anything-llm](https://github.com/Mintplex-Labs/anything-llm)
+- **Stars:** 53.3k ⭐
+- **Language:** JavaScript
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** Jan 14, 2026
+- **Category:** rag, self-hosted-chat
+- **Best for:** internal restaurant knowledge assistants and embeddable guest chat
+
