+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI tools for restaurants in 2026?
+For restaurant-specific operations, start with `TastyIgniter` for ordering and reservations, then add `Chatwoot`, `Rasa`, or `Pipecat` for guest-facing AI. For document-heavy workflows, `PaddleOCR`, `Surya`, and `Marker` are the strongest menu and PDF ingestion options. For forecasting, use `Prophet`, `Darts`, or `StatsForecast`.
+
+### Are there any truly restaurant-native open source AI repos on GitHub?
+Very few. That is the biggest gap this research exposed. Most practical solutions for restaurants are assembled from adjacent OSS categories such as conversational AI, OCR, retrieval, and forecasting. This README intentionally documents that gap instead of pretending there are dozens of mature restaurant-only AI repositories.
+
+### Which GitHub tools are best for digitizing restaurant menus with AI?
+`PaddleOCR`, `Surya`, `Tesseract`, `Marker`, and `Unstructured` are the best starting points. Use `PaddleOCR` or `Surya` when layout and multilingual support matter. Use `Marker` when the goal is PDF-to-Markdown for retrieval or RAG. Use `Tesseract` when you want a well-known low-level OCR engine.
+
+### What is the best open source stack for a restaurant chatbot?
+A common stack is `Rasa` or `Flowise` or `Langflow` for orchestration, `Qdrant` or `Weaviate` for retrieval, and `Chatwoot` for human handoff. If voice is important, add `Pipecat`. If you want a faster all-in-one self-hosted UI, try `AnythingLLM`.
+
+### Which AI repos help restaurants forecast demand and staffing?
+`Prophet` is the easiest entry point. `Darts` is better when you want many models and anomaly detection. `StatsForecast` and `MLForecast` are strong for large multi-store forecasting, while `AutoTS` is useful for broad automated model search.
+
+### Can I self-host all of the tools in this list?
+They all exist as public GitHub repositories, but licensing and deployment models differ. Most are clearly self-hostable. Review license details carefully for repositories with mixed or non-standard licensing, especially before commercial deployment.
